# Reusable story styles

Typography update, 20 September: [bundled story fonts](STORY_FONT_VERIFICATION.md)
extend the parameter-only format with bounded, licensed font references. Text,
depth and recipe styles retain those references; unknown editions are preserved
but unavailable to apply. Font bytes remain in project assets, outside shared
style files. Full per-image/folder composition remains open.

Update, 20 September: **For this story** now presents the three
[authored story recipes](STORY_RECIPE_VERIFICATION.md). **Photo looks** retains
the original color-only catalog and its immutable revisions. Recipes introduce
the bounded `storyDesign` component; Depth cover targets the cover photo and
requires its subject mask. The original storage/file/privacy contracts below
continue to apply.

Recorded 2026-09-17. This implements the local story-style library from §5A of
the [migration plan](../MIGRATION_PLAN.md). Finished launch recipes, asset packs,
the remaining per-image/bulk story-style components remain required work. The
[photo-look adapter](PHOTO_LOOK_EXECUTION_VERIFICATION.md) now applies frozen
photo-color components to image sets and folder jobs, with own-photo previews,
strength, scoped application and recovery. Typography, layout, cutouts and
grouped story execution are still required. The later
[legacy recipe migration](LEGACY_PRESET_MIGRATION.md) preserves flat recipes
and destination definitions. The full preset and production gates remain open.

## Mobile workflow

**Look** has For this story, Saved and Recent views. Saved can filter Favorites
or My styles. The three initial curated looks use previews of the current
photos. Tap a card, adjust photo-color strength where meaningful, choose This
slide or Whole story, then Apply. Multiple choices within the sheet produce
one undo entry. Cancel restores its opening document.
Apply stays disabled while the selected document is waiting for its preview
or rendering has failed. The sheet exposes the error and Retry preview; failed
previews do not enter project recovery as committed edits.

A favorite is saved from its card without committing the image preview.
Recent lists the last twelve applied revisions; browsing and saving alone do
not count as an application. The library retains revisions explicitly and
shows the selected revision. Removing a library entry leaves definitions
already copied into projects intact.

**Save my style** asks for a name and offers Look, Text style, Layout, Output,
Cutout finish and Depth title.
Look and Text style are selected initially. Color capture uses the current
slide's first photo, including its resolved corrections. Text capture keeps
typography and color. Layout captures supported photo-print constraints and
arrangement rules. Output captures the selected shape and PNG/JPEG format.
Cutout finish captures outline/shadow parameters from an explicitly chosen
subject on the current slide. It applies to existing subject selections in the
chosen scope, preserves their masks/crops and declares `cutout-effects-v1`.
Photos without masks are unchanged; scopes with no selected subject are rejected.
Connected copies change on both of their slides, as the preview sheet explains.
See [finish rendering and verification](CUTOUT_EFFECTS_VERIFICATION.md).
Depth title captures built-in type, color/shadow, photo-relative placement and
the original-photo/page background choice from a selected title. It excludes
the title's words and source-specific subject mask. See the
[depth-style addition](#depth-style-addition-20-september-2026) below.
Output settings affect the whole story even when appearance is scoped to a
slide; the sheet says this before applying.

The capture contains no original image, source identity/name, source-specific
mask, caption text, focal point, exact crop or manually positioned photo frame. A
depth style does include its title box relative to the photo. The
style name is explicitly supplied by the user; it does not default to a
private project title. Existing crops and mask repairs survive application.
Layout replacements keep adjusted positions unless the user explicitly
unchecks that choice. Save a project backup to retain private content.

Scoped photo looks now have a separate `appearanceBase` in the image node.
The resolver combines shared appearance, scoped look, manual node corrections,
then local appearance overrides. Selecting a different scoped look changes
its base without erasing manual corrections. **Use the story look** explicitly
clears the scoped base and local color corrections, and is reversible.
Optional per-slide layout constraints similarly prevent a style applied to one
page from changing another page's later reflow.

Style cards are loaded in groups of twelve. An intersection observer queues
only nearby thumbnails, cancels work for departed cards and releases their
object URLs and retained preview planes. Changing views or closing the sheet
also releases those resources. The shared scheduler still admits and copies
source data only when a worker has capacity, with active artwork ahead of
these thumbnail jobs.

## File and storage contracts

[The style model](../src/styles/model.js) uses declarative
`tiny-image-star/style` version 1 data: stable ID, immutable integer revision,
name/description, engine-version and compositor allowlists, required
capabilities, supported photo counts/output shapes, exposed strength control,
project-seed policy and selected components. The current consumer accepts
6–12-photo story projects with portrait/tall outputs.

All fields, enums, numbers, typography and layout constraints are validated.
Imports are at most 32 KiB; file size is checked before reading, and the parsed
schema is checked before a preview is rendered.
The schema has no executable expressions or remote-fetch fields. Version 1
accepts an empty asset list and built-in font choices; custom fonts and other
asset packs require a supported, licensed packaging path before export.
Unknown required capabilities or engine versions can be stored as identifiable
unavailable styles, but cannot be applied. Invalid schemas and unimplemented
component types are rejected. The original file remains unchanged.

**Export style** downloads the selected definition as `.tstyle` JSON.
**Import style** validates and stores a file, then requires an explicit preview
and Apply. The same ID/revision with different settings is a conflict, including
across tabs. Imported files cannot replace an installed built-in revision.
Exact duplicates are safe; a new revision coexists with the previous one.
Applied definitions are copied into project history and recovery, rather than
looked up dynamically during rendering.

[The library store](../src/styles/store.js) uses strict IndexedDB transactions
in `tiny-image-star.styles`, capped at 100 stored revisions and 2 MiB of library
records. Definitions are copied before asynchronous storage. Favorite and
recent-use metadata may change without mutating a definition. Recent-use order
is monotonic even when two applications share a clock tick. BroadcastChannel
notifies other app tabs to reread committed data.

Quota failure leaves existing records untouched and can be retried. Clearing
local data includes styles and prevents pending same-page saves from
recreating the cleared library. Unknown/corrupt records are omitted from the
usable list with a visible warning; their raw records remain available in the
private `.tstar` backup. The private backup includes styles, original project
assets and existing legacy recipes. Style-file sharing contains only the
validated style definition.

The existing image/set/folder recipes and destination choices now have a
[private compatibility migration](LEGACY_PRESET_MIGRATION.md), preserving IDs,
legacy operation order and originals through verified reopening. These use a
separate execution format in the same store. The active legacy catalog now
commits atomically with its definitions, rejects stale edits/deletes and retains
conflicting drafts for a new-copy save. Future or malformed catalog records
remain read-only and available in private backups. Story style files are not
advertised as bulk-image presets until that consumer is implemented and verified.
The private compatibility recipes now support [revision-specific image choices
and recovery](RECIPE_SELECTION_VERIFICATION.md), including a folder selector
that retains its saved version after library edits or removal.

## Evidence

[The original nine style-model tests](../tests/styles.test.mjs), alongside the 24 existing
model/layout tests, cover selected-field capture, privacy exclusions, exact
file roundtrips, scoped current/future reflow, strength endpoints, manual
correction precedence, crop/mask preservation, explicit position reset,
immutable copied definitions, multi-preview undo/cancel, missing fonts,
unsupported requirements/engines, malformed files and old static layouts.

[Real-browser storage checks](../tests/style-storage.browser.mjs) cover frozen
submission, two-tab revision races and notifications, quota rollback/retry,
duplicate/revision identity, built-in impersonation rejection, the 100-revision
limit, unknown-data preservation, private backup equality, monotonic recent
ordering and clearing during a pending save. A separate injected UI refusal
checks that a failed preview does not replace the previous selection or enter
Recent; that particular failure test uses thumbnail/preview doubles.

[The mobile workflow](../tests/story-workspace.browser.mjs) uses the real
published Pillow engine for favorite/save/export/remove/import/apply/undo,
missing-requirement labels, applied-copy preservation, scoped corrections,
and existing recovery/export paths. An 18-style library checks twelve-card
paging, offscreen thumbnail release, retained-preview cleanup on close and
200% text width. The actual `.tstyle` download is parsed and compared to the
saved definition, including private-data exclusion assertions.
Injected source-read and synchronous queue-admission failures go through the
scene client, disable Apply, leave the stored caption unchanged, and support
retry then cancel. This includes a failure before a worker task is created;
the sheet must still expose its error and Retry action.
The synchronous-admission regression was first run against the prior built
artifact: it reproduced a hidden Retry action while the canvas reported the
injected error. The corrected source passes the complete story workflow with
both failure paths. This is a recovery regression, not a throughput benchmark.
[The phone screenshot](research/2026-09-17/story-styles-phone.png) was inspected
after previews decoded. These checks do not establish touch-device latency,
screen-reader coverage, font licensing, perceptual recipe quality or sustained
phone memory/thermal limits.

Final verification for this step on 2026-09-17:

- `npm run verify`: existing engine/application checks, eleven scheduler tests,
  and all 33 project/layout/style tests passed.
- The focused source story browser workflow passed with both preview failure
  injections and the full style, layout, recovery and export sequence.
- `node scripts/assemble-pages.mjs _site` and
  `node scripts/check-pages-artifact.mjs _site`: passed, 125 packaged files.
- `TINY_IMAGE_STAR_BROWSER_ROOT=_site npm run verify:browser`: passed against
  the rebuilt artifact, including concurrent image/text/scene processing,
  storage races and failures, story styles, existing image/batch/folder flows,
  theme and 200% text checks. No unexpected external requests or page errors.
- `npm run migration:check`: both inventoried endpoints and all 33 immutable
  parity cases passed specification checks; this did not rerun performance
  qualification or replace the recorded failing microbenchmark.
- Documentation links and `git diff --check`: passed.

The working tree remains uncommitted and the site has not been deployed by
this step. Release-revision evidence and the remaining migration gates must
still be completed.

## Depth-style addition, 20 September 2026

**Save my style → Depth title** records a `depthTitle` component with schema 1
and the required `depth-title-v1` capability. The existing version-one style
envelope, copied revisions, transactional library and bounded file workflow are
retained. Readers that cannot handle this component reject it; they must not
silently remove the effect. Custom fonts still require a licensed asset pack.

Apply to **This slide** or **Whole story** affects only photos that already
have a subject mask. The preview explains the eligible/skipped counts and
shared connected subjects. A scope without any eligible photo is unavailable.
Existing title words, captions, source photos, crops and mask repairs stay in
place. New titles use the destination story's name and remain editable in Text.
A style never supplies the author's private words or reuses their mask on a
different photo.

**Keep adjusted positions**, enabled initially, keeps all existing title
frames and their output-specific/local overrides. Unchecking it applies the
saved relative title box and removes position overrides on affected titles;
local words and opacity stay intact. New titles always start in the saved box.
Connected subjects get one shared title change across both neighboring slides.
Depth settings are independent of photo-color strength.

Five additional model tests cover privacy and JSON roundtrips; photo-relative
capture with local overrides; source/crop/mask preservation; scoped and connected
application; explicit reset; immutable revisions, undo and reload; custom-font
refusal; and malformed/private/executable fields and numeric bounds. The mobile
depth browser workflow now exercises capture and actual `.tstyle` download,
cancel, reuse on existing/new titles, preserved words, keep/reset positions,
undo/redo, exact rendered reload and no-mask availability. The existing depth
export checks then compare the saved-document render with ordered PNG output.

This completes reusable depth-title parameters inside stories. It does not
finish the authored Depth cover recipe or enable depth styles in independent
image/folder jobs. Their consumers, automatic selection, production fonts,
real-photo quality and physical mobile qualification remain open.

The [20 September verification record](research/2026-09-20/depth-styles/README.md)
retains passing source and packaged Chromium suites, all 124 deterministic
checks, 33/33 adapter comparisons, coverage, source/build inventories and the
visually inspected phone screenshot. It records the dirty-worktree and prior
benchmark identity limits separately from observed test outcomes.

## Multiple export shapes — 20 September 2026

Including Output now retains the explicit Portrait/Tall/Both export selection
and format, separately from the shape currently being previewed. New definitions
with a shapes array declare `export-variants-v1`. Their validated file roundtrip
and actual mobile save/apply/reload workflow preserve both shapes without copying
photos or changing source corrections. Legacy output styles keep their original
single-shape semantics and replace a later multi-shape selection when applied.
The [export verification](STORY_EXPORT_VERIFICATION.md) records the copied-plan,
retry, cancellation and concurrency boundaries. Grouped collections and the
complete independent-image/folder style consumers remain open.
